timeout behavior must be experimentally validated with multiple failure modes to confirm fail-closed behavior. Furthermore, rigorous OS containment design requires distinct identities for human users, the master agent, and worker agents, enforcing strict privilege separation.\n\n\nLevel 2A safety requires all twelve specified conditions; a single file alone isn't sufficient. Operation awareness must be implemented for containerization tools, categorizing specific commands with varying safety levels, with many being hard-denied. Git commands also require operation awareness, applying local project restrictions to various actions and hard-denying dangerous operations.\n\nPolicy files, including configuration and rules, must be protected by the gateway and filesystem ownership/permissions. The rule distribution requires a matrix to avoid rule duplication; preserving the existing authoritative location of each rule is important. Strict phasing is mandated for implementation, including rigorous testing before any active enforcement or OS-level hardening.\n\n\nExperimental verification of native hook timeout behavior is required, explicitly not relying on documentation. The test plan involves controlled experiments within a read-only environment, covering various hook failure scenarios: allow, deny, crash, malformed JSON, timeout, and process kill. The goal is to determine if a timeout results in a denial or tool execution.  The existence of the config directory and previous crash behavior are relevant to the test design. A test harness is being considered. The hooks.json file and \"matcher\" configuration within are being examined for hook registration of a dummy test hook and the implications of the \"matcher\" value are being considered.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"import sys\\nimport json\\nimport os\\n\\nmode_file = \\\"/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/hook_experiment/mode.txt\\\"\\nlog_file = \\\"/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/hook_experiment/hook_runs.log\\\"\\n\\nmode = \\\"allow\\\"\\nif os.path.exists(mode_file):\\n    with open(mode_file, \\\"r\\\") as f:\\n        mode = f.read().strip()\\n\\nwith open(log_file, \\\"a\\\") as f:\\n    f.write(f\\\"HOOK RUNNING with mode: {mode}\\\\n\\\")\\n\\nif mode == \\\"allow\\\":\\n    print(json.dumps({\\\"decision\\\": \\\"allow\\\"}))\\n    sys.exit(0)\\nelif mode == \\\"deny\\\":\\n    print(json.dumps({\\\"decision\\\": \\\"deny\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Controlled experiment: Deny triggered\\\"}))\\n    sys.exit(0)\\nelif mode == \\\"crash\\\":\\n    with open(log_file, \\\"a\\\") as f:\\n        f.write(\\\"HOOK CRASHING with exit 1\\\\n\\\")\\n    sys.stderr.write(\\\"Simulated fatal exception in hook\\\\n\\\")\\n    sys.exit(1)\\nelif mode == \\\"malformed\\\":\\n    print(\\\"THIS_IS_NOT_VALID_JSON{decision: allow, broken\\\")\\n    sys.exit(0)\\nelif mode == \\\"timeout\\\":\\n    import time\\n    with open(log_file, \\\"a\\\") as f:\\n        f.write(\\\"HOOK SLEEPING for 10s to trigger timeout\\\\n\\\")\\n    time.sleep(10)\\n    print(json.dumps({\\\"decision\\\": \\\"allow\\\"}))\\n    sys.exit(0)\\nelif mode == \\\"kill\\\":\\n    import signal\\n    with open(log_file, \\\"a\\\") as f:\\n        f.write(\\\"HOOK KILLING SELF with SIGKILL\\\\n\\\")\\n    os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGKILL)\\nelse:\\n    print(json.dumps({\\\"decision\\\": \\\"allow\\\"}))\\n    sys.exit(0)\\n\"","Description":"\"Test hook script supporting 6 test modes\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/hook_experiment/test_hook_behavior.py\"","toolAction":"\"Creating test hook script\"","toolSummary":"\"Write test_hook_behavior.py\""}}]}
